<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Passavant</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1263 CE (approx.) · capital Passavant</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Frankish barony in the Mani held by the marshals of Achaea, whose castle of Passava watched over the unruly Maniots.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1220 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-neuilly">John of Neuilly</a>, hereditary marshal of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>, received a fief at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> peninsula and built a castle there called Passavant, now Passava, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gytheio">Gytheio</a>. Its purpose was to control the independent-minded population of the Mani and the route into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The barony was small but held by one of the great officers of the principality, the marshal, who commanded the army. Its lords ruled from the castle over Greek villages, alongside the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> further south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> in 1259, he ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, and Passavant was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> soon after, around 1263. The Neuilly heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-passavant">Margaret of Passavant</a> later claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-passavant.jpg" alt="Barony of Passavant" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: Constantine ✍ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1263 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Passavant</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John of Neuilly</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Passava</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=barony-of-passavant" aria-label="Barony of Passavant on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5245 854 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barony of Passavant lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5565 1110L5530 1071M5565 1110L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5530 1071h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5565 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5565 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=barony-of-passavant">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

